    {
      "anchor": "backfilling",
      "kind": "heading",
      "level": 1,
      "text": "Backfilling"
    },
    {
      "anchor": "backfilling",
      "kind": "prose",
      "text": "Backfilling is a feature that allows you to start servers without guaranteeing how long they will be running. The servers defined as “backfilling servers” behave the same as normal servers, except for the following:"
    },
    {
      "anchor": "backfilling",
      "kind": "prose",
      "text": "they occupy the free resources currently available in the cloud"
    },
    {
      "anchor": "backfilling",
      "kind": "prose",
      "text": "they can be stopped at an arbitrary moment if demand for resources appears by normal servers"
    },
    {
      "anchor": "backfilling",
      "kind": "prose",
      "text": "instead of shutdown they can be dynamically downsized in terms of CPU and memory if demand for resources appear by normal servers"
    },
    {
      "anchor": "backfilling",
      "kind": "prose",
      "text": "they are billed at a separate discount burst rate. No CPU and MEM subscriptions are allowed for the resources consumed by the backfilling servers."
    },
    {
      "anchor": "backfilling",
      "kind": "prose",
      "text": "To use backfilling servers your account must be granted special permission."
    },
    {
      "anchor": "checking-for-available-backfilling-resources",
      "kind": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "Checking For Available Backfilling Resources"
    },
    {
      "anchor": "checking-for-available-backfilling-resources",
      "kind": "prose",
      "text": "If your account is granted permission to run a backfilling server the capabilities API (see Capabilities) call will show additional information regarding backfilling capacity. The free_resources object contains three objects, which show what are the maximum resources that can be used by a single backfilling server."
    },
    {
      "anchor": "checking-for-available-backfilling-resources",
      "kind": "prose",
      "text": "Here is an example capabilities call from a backfilling user:"
    },
    {
      "anchor": "checking-for-available-backfilling-resources",
      "kind": "prose",
      "text": "The following list summarises the information given by each free_resources object:"
    },
    {
      "anchor": "checking-for-available-backfilling-resources",
      "kind": "prose",
      "text": "total"
    },
    {
      "anchor": "checking-for-available-backfilling-resources",
      "kind": "prose",
      "text": "This object shows what are the maximum CPU and memory available for backfilling servers. Note that these maximum values may be available on different hosts, so it may not be possible to start a server with this parameters."
    },
    {
      "anchor": "checking-for-available-backfilling-resources",
      "kind": "prose",
      "text": "most_free_by_cpu"
    },
    {
      "anchor": "checking-for-available-backfilling-resources",
      "kind": "prose",
      "text": "This object shows what is the maximum CPU available for a single server, and what is the amount of memory that is associated with this CPU. Use this object if you need as much CPU as possible."
    },
    {
      "anchor": "checking-for-available-backfilling-resources",
      "kind": "prose",
      "text": "most_free_by_memory"
    },
    {
      "anchor": "checking-for-available-backfilling-resources",
      "kind": "prose",
      "text": "This object shows what is the maximum memory available for a single server, and what is the amount of CPU associated with it. If you need as much RAM as possible in your server, use this object."
    },
    {
      "anchor": "checking-for-available-backfilling-resources",
      "kind": "prose",
      "text": "In general, the maximum memory and maximum CPU will not be available on a single physical host, so you should use either most_free_by_cpu or most_free_by_memory depending on whether your workload needs CPU or memory."
    },
